#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228859 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/id #uuid "301e7fe6-6892-5e0a-9368-b3857ae8fa39" :question/math-academy-id "e-21649" :question/problem "Given that $\\displaystyle J(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x,$ calculate $J'(t)$ for $t\\in\\mathbb R$ by differentiating under the integral sign." :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as differentiating under the integral sign.\n\nWe wish to compute $J'(t),$ where\n\n$$\nJ(t)=\\int_{0}^{1} (\\cos t+2x)^2\\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\nJ'(t) = \\int_{0}^{1} \\frac{\\partial}{\\partial t}\\Bigl[(\\cos t+2x)^2\\Bigr]\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)^{2}] & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x) \\\\  & =2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{J}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}-2(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{1}+{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nJ'(t) =-2\\sin t\\,(\\cos t+1).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186228874 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/id #uuid "830924ac-a51c-505a-b5b4-c8a4ad916dfe" :question/math-academy-id "e-21732" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_1(t) = \\int_0^1 t\\cos x\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in \\mathbb R$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_2(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in (0,1)$\n\n- $\\displaystyle F_3(t) = \\int_0^1 \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}\\,\\textrm{d} x, \\quad t \\in [0,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R = [a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t) = \\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\nF'(t) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}\\int_{a}^b f(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x\n=\n\\int_{a}^b \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}(x,t)\\,\\textrm{d} x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\nf_1(x,t) = t\\cos x.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in \\mathbb R$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq \\mathbb R,$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_1$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_1}{\\partial t} = \\cos x\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_1(t)$ for all real $t.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\nf_2(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{|x - t|}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq (0,1),$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_2$ is undefined when $x = t,$ since the denominator vanishes. Thus, $f_2$ is not continuous on $R,$ and differentiating under the integral sign is *not* valid.\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\nf_3(x,t) = \\dfrac{1}{1 + (x - t)^2}.\n$$\n\nWe fix $t_0 \\in (0,1)$ and choose $c < t_0 < d$ such that $[c,d] \\subseteq [0,1],$ so $R = [0,1] \\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that $f_3$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f_3}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{2(x - t)}{(1 + (x - t)^2)^2}\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at $t_0.$ And since our choice of $t_0$ was arbitrary, we can compute $F'_3(t)$ for all $t \\in [0,1].$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186229605 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2(1+t^3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c56f45b8-10dd-51a2-b414-43379bcbec60" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6t^2(1+t^3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325519} :answer-field/id #uuid "958f7ba9-45b9-5cf0-95b5-ea32ae4d67d0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186228085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "635c83de-1981-509e-9e67-8590851bc8fa" :question/math-academy-id "q-332944" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(2x+{t}^{3})^{2}] & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(2x+{t}^{3}) \\\\  & =2(2x+{t}^{3})\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}6{t}^{2}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(2x+{t}^{3})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}2x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{3}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =1+{t}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{3}).\n$$"}]}
